User change: the front-end "Consumer decision" section should present 8 options for who sends live traffic through the front-end, not one pick. Keep your other output sections as they are.

Structure it as:

A) 3 SAFE options. They are usable today with defaults off and fail-closed behaviour. Examples: a shadow/observe-only hook that logs the decision but always forwards; a CLI wrapper you invoke explicitly; an advisory UserPromptSubmit hook that only attaches context.

B) 5 NOT-YET-SAFE options. Each can serve answers locally or act autonomously, so each carries real risk now. Each must become safe only through measured training/evaluation gates. Examples to consider: a local answer served in place of Claude for in-distribution prompts; verbatim cache replay; tool-route auto-execution; a local model drafting answers that Claude only edits; a per-skill local takeover; any others you judge better.

For EACH of the 8 options give:
- how it works: the consumer, and who assembles context and forwards to Claude;
- what it touches (file:line);
- the failure mode / blast radius if wrong;
- for the 5 risky ones: a concrete promotion ladder of shadow → canary → on. Every rung needs a numeric exit gate under RD-LOG Iron rules: tune/held/confirm splits, Holm, TOST ±0.01, KEEP. Include wrong-local-rate ceilings with upper CI bounds, ≥30 rows, ≥3 sessions, judge decoy-reject ≥0.70, and minimum shadow volume. Also give the training/data each rung needs, and an automatic kill-switch/demotion rule.

Then state your recommended starting option (a safe one) and which risky option is the best first candidate for training toward safety, and why. You may extend the word limit to ~1800 words to fit this.

Your report came back without the 8-option consumer section the user asked for. My earlier message probably reached you after you had finished. Please send ONLY that section now; keep everything else as you already reported it.

Who sends live traffic through the front-end? Give 8 options:

A) 3 SAFE options. They must be usable today with defaults off and fail-closed behaviour. Your `python -m llm ask` CLI wrapper can be one of them. Candidates for the others: a shadow/observe-only path that logs the decision but always forwards; a context-only UserPromptSubmit hook, scoped to this repo's .claude/settings.json and never global, with a hard timeout under 10 s.

B) 5 NOT-YET-SAFE options. Each one either serves answers locally or acts autonomously, so each is risky today. Each must become safe only through measured training and evaluation. Consider: local answers for in-distribution prompts in place of Claude; verbatim verified-cache replay; tool-route auto-execution (calc/units/csp); the local model drafting and Claude only editing or verifying; a per-skill local takeover; a hook that substitutes answers. Use others if you judge them better.

For EACH of the 8 options, give:
- how it works: the consumer, who assembles context, who forwards to Claude;
- what it touches (file:line);
- its failure mode and blast radius.

For EACH of the 5 risky options, also give a promotion ladder shadow → canary (x% of traffic) → on. Each rung needs:
- numeric exit gates under RD-LOG Iron rules: cluster-blocked tune/held/confirm, Holm, TOST ±0.01, KEEP; a wrong-answer ceiling using the UPPER CI bound; ≥30 rows; ≥3 clusters; ≥2 worlds; judge decoy-reject ≥0.70; a minimum shadow volume;
- the training/data needed to get there;
- an automatic demotion/kill-switch rule.

End with:
- your recommended starting option (a safe one);
- the risky option that is the best first candidate to train toward safety, and why;
- what blocks ALL risky options today: the single world, and decoy-reject at .60.

Keep it to about 1500 words.

resp.t:
